   CPWATCH 4.3                - 2 -            02 December 1984

1  CPWATCH                                          DESCRIPTION
0                          DESCRIPTION
+                          ___________

-  CURRENT STATE
+  _____________
0     Operational,  stabilized.  Running  on  a 3083-E16  under
   VM/SP 3.1 HPO 3.2.

-  OPERATION
+  _________
0     The  monitoring machine  must  have  class E  privileges.
   Command  "CPWATCHX"  will build  a  load  deck and  IPL  it.
   CPWATCHX uses VMFLOAD to build the  deck; it examines the VM
   system/release level using  DIAG 0, and selects  its control
   file from the list in CPWLVL  DATA to be appropriate for the
   system running.  'DMKLD00E  LOADER' must be available  on an
   accessed disk.
0     It is easiest to AUTOLOG the monitoring machine at VM/370
   IPL time.
0     Note that this program examines CP's linked lists asynch-
   ronously, and  interruptible.  Each examination is  *not* at
   an instant of time, but spread over some period of time; the
   data it receives may be inconsistent.  To minimize this, the
   monitoring machine should run  at maximum priority (priority
   = 1)  and be  FAVORed.  The machine  will run  compute bound
   when it runs, but that is  for short intervals (order of 200
   milliseconds) every 10 seconds; the performance impact, even
   on online  applications, should be  minimal, and  the output
   will be much better.

-  Access
+  ______
0|    CPWATCH can  be accessed  using the  CP DIAL  command, or
 | from a CMS machine using VMCF.
 |
 |    To access using DIAL:
 |         CP DIAL userid
 | Where "userid"  is the name  of the virtual  machine running
 | the monitor, usually 'CPWATCH'.
 |
 |    Using VMCF, the CMS MODULE "CPWCMS" is used:
 |        CPWCMS Ýscreen-name¨ Ý( Ý SERVER userid ¨¨
 | Note that module "CPWCMS" has the userid "CPWATCH" wired in,
 | but it can be overridden from  the command line. (It can, of
 | course, be changed permanently by recompiling CPWCMS).

-
0  CPWATCH 4.3                - 3 -            02 December 1984

1  CPWATCH                                          DESCRIPTION
0|    The first screen displayed shows a menu of available com-
 | mands.

-  FUTURE DEVELOPMENT
+  __________________
-     Hard copy output  is intended, sometime.  It  would be in
   the form of screen images written on the printer.
0     Direct display to  the console of the  monitoring Virtual
   Machine, using the  EDGAR interface, or the  3270 SIO inter-
   face of VM/SP,  is planned.  This will  allow someone logged
   on the monitoring machine to control CP's tuning parameters,
   and see the result (if any) of each change.

