   CPWATCH 4.3                - 13 -           02 December 1984

1  CPWATCH                                          MAINTENANCE
0  CPWLVL Notes
+  ____________
0     CPWLVL is a newly added module which reads the system and
   maintenance level of  CP using DIAGnose 0,  and formats this
   information in human-readable form.  It will then optionally
   look up this level in file  "CPWLVL DATA" to find the appro-
   priate  CNTRL file  for  loading CPWATCH,  or  will write  a
   record to  this file to  record a  new control file.   If it
   fails to  find the system  or version  in the data  file, it
   will set the CNTRL file name to "UNKNOWN".
0     The data  file record  selected has  the "SYSTEM  ID" (eg
   VM/370, VM/SP) field which exactly  matches the one produced
   by DIAGnose. Of  all records whose SYSTEM ID  match, the one
   with the highest release, version, and plc level record that
   is less than or equal to the DIAGnosed one is selected.
   Displaying the current level:
        CPWLVL TYPE
0  Stacking the control file information:
        CPWLVL STACK
   the stacked data takes the form:
        * <cntrl-file> <system-id>
0  Updating the data file:
        CPWLVL WRITE <cntrl-file>

-| CPWCMS Notes
+  ____________
 |
 |
 |    CPWCMS is a transient-area routine, so it can be executed
 | from almost anywhere. It may be  useful to set it to execute
 | from a PFkey in favourite editor profile.
 |
 |    Note  that  the  SERVER  userid,   and  the  last  screen
 | requested, are  "stickey" - as long  as CPWCMS is  not over-
 | written in  the transient area,  the values are  passed from
 | execution to execution.
 |
 |    CPWATCH support of VMCF stripped down to cost the minimum
 | of overhead.  If CPWATCH  thinks a  userid is  listening, it
 | sends it  updates.  If  one is rejected  for any  reason, it
 | quits sending until the user asks for it again.
 |
 |    The VMCF  protocol used  is subject  to a  possible major
 | revision in  the future,  if non-Model  2 screen  support is
 | introduced into CPWATCH.
 |
 |    There is one known bug in  CPWCMS. If CP takes the screen
 | format  when  a  PFkey  is hit  (e.g.  to  display  messages
 | received), and  while the  screen is in  CP format  the user
 | enters a  "3215-mode" data  line, CPWCMS  cannot complete  a
 | fullscreen read, and does not pass an attention interrupt to
 | CMS. The  result is a  paralysed terminal.  The  bypass cur-
0  CPWATCH 4.3                - 14 -           02 December 1984

1  CPWATCH                                          MAINTENANCE
0| rently available is to (1) use PA1  to enter CP, (2) use the
 | CP EXT command to enter DEBUG,  and (3) use the HX immediate
 | command  to terminate  execution. Since  CPWCMS has  already
 | trapped  the  KILLEX  routine, this  will  cause  CPWCMS  to
 | restore CMS's PSWs and allow the abend to proceed.

-  Module Descriptions
+  ___________________
-       CPWSUP - Supervisor and I/O handler.
+       CPWSUP
0       CPWDSP -  Display definitions,  and service  func-
